WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:14.488
Imagine a law that lets only one bus company run in your town. Fares are high and there is no choice. If the government removes that rule, new bus firms can enter, and fares may fall.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:27.198
Deregulation means removing or reducing government rules and controls on firms and markets. It is a supply-side policy: it aims to raise competition and the productive capacity of the economy.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:40.169
Letting more firms, including foreign firms, into a market is deregulation. So is relaxing employment rules. Adding a minimum wage, licences or safety laws is the opposite.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:56.300
Step one, define it. Step two, chain an advantage. Fewer rules mean lower costs and easier entry, so there is more competition and investment, then lower prices, more jobs and growth.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:01:06.649
On a diagram, lower costs and new firms increase supply. Supply shifts right, from S to S one. Price falls and quantity rises.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:20.410
Step three, chain a disadvantage. Rules often protect people. Remove them, and workers may lose job security, conditions may become unsafe, and pollution may rise.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:37.557
Step four, apply it to the sector named in the case. Step five, for Assess, weigh it up. Will firms really hire? What protections are lost? Would other policies work better? Then judge.

00:01:38.680 --> 00:02:02.475
Here is a real question from November 2020. South Korea's unemployment for people aged fifteen to twenty-nine rose to eleven point six per cent. Experts advised cutting employment rules in finance, transport and tourism. Assess the effectiveness of deregulation in reducing unemployment. Nine marks.

00:02:03.040 --> 00:02:11.389
Pick out the data: eleven point six per cent of young people unemployed, reports of too many rules, and three named service sectors.

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:23.620
Employment rules add to the cost and risk of hiring. Relax them, and tourism and transport firms find it cheaper to hire, so they take on more staff, including young people.

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:34.380
Removing rules that limit entry into finance lets new firms in. They need staff, so jobs are created, and more competition may lower prices.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:51.630
But firms may keep the savings as profit instead of hiring. Lost rules can mean less job security or unsafe work. And youth unemployment may have other causes, like a skills mismatch, so training could work better.

00:02:52.200 --> 00:03:10.227
Judge. Deregulation could cut youth unemployment in these sectors if rules really are the main barrier to hiring. But the effect is uncertain, so it works best alongside other supply-side policies. This explanation is by olevels dot com, not an official mark scheme.

00:03:11.360 --> 00:03:23.193
A common mistake is confusing deregulation with privatisation. Privatisation sells state-owned firms. Deregulation removes rules.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:31.593
Another is picking a new rule, such as a minimum wage, as an example. That adds a rule, so it is the opposite.

00:03:32.720 --> 00:03:52.344
Now you try. An example country removes the rule that allowed only two taxi firms in its capital. It also relaxes rules on drivers' working hours. Explain one advantage and one disadvantage. Pause here and work it out.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:04:06.858
Advantage: new taxi firms can enter, so supply shifts right. More competition means lower fares and more rides, and the new firms create jobs for drivers.

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:18.741
Disadvantage: with no limit on working hours, drivers may work very long shifts. Tired drivers are less safe, so workers and passengers lose protection.

00:04:19.320 --> 00:04:31.730
Remember: define deregulation, chain an advantage through costs and competition, chain a disadvantage through lost protection, and apply it to the case. Now try the practice questions.
